>Raving Militant Feminists are forever saying that porn plays up to the
>fantasy of men dominating women. My point is that this is clearly
>bollocks.

I don't think it's necessarily this that worries most feminists, it's
certainly not what worries me.  The linkage between pornography and
sexual violence is controversial.  Somewhat less controversial,
however, is the connection between consumption of pornography. Baxter
(New Scientist 5/5/90 --- I know it's out of date but it's not my
field) described a study where _both_ men and women who had been
exposed to pornography came up with a lower sentence for a rapist in a
mock trial in which they were jurors.  Exposure to `harder core'
produced a yet shorter sentences.  

If I were convinced that a man who raped a woman would, on average,
face a sentence which most people would consider fair for a man who
committed a similar assault on another man, and we lived in a society
in which exploitation of women was at least as rare as exploitation of
other races, then perhaps we could be less careful about pornography.

Perhaps this is the clue to the apparently OK situation in Sweden.
Although they produce hard-core, perhaps the fairly egalitarian
society renders it (relatively) harmless, as it would in an ideal
world.  From the limited samples I've seen, though undoubtedly more
explicit than it's typical equivalents elsewhere, I would have said it
was less exploitative in appearance.

Unfortunately there is an appetite for darker stuff, else the Police
chief in the recent Moroccan court case wouldn't have made so much
money by selling videos of the hundreds of rapes he had committed.

					... John Woods
