>task of reviewing every single article in USENET is impossible.  [...]

Yes - reviewing the contents of all groups is impossible.  Making no attempt
at control, however, is foolish from a legal standpoint.

Certain newsgroups are collecting points for porn/obscenity.  If it someone
were to post a "kiddie-porn" image to "comp.lang.c" (or any other group) the
peer pressure of the group would self-police the action.  The action is an
exception.  You could argue to a legal official (or jury) that the group
actually discourages such activity, even though they/you do not enforce
strict content control.  

However, it can readily be seen that groups like
"alt.binaries.pictures.erotica" serve the purpose of collecting erotic
images of all forms. The base content of the group is erotica (and maybe
obscenity, depending on your state/local laws).  If you cannot control
access by minors to groups such as "alt.binaries.pictures.erotica" (or even
"alt.sex.stories") and you have laws dealing with the dissemination of such
materials to minors, you have something to worry about.  Controlling access,
either to your client-systems or to the major offending newsgroups
(eliminating them being one form of access-control) are currently the only
options. 

If you are a Usenet News admin and are complacent in the assumption that "I
am protected - I am just managing a system for ...", check with a lawyer
first. I firmly believe the responsiblity will mainly rest with the system
administrator (or whoever is at the point of "control" of Usenet at that
site).  We are talking about criminal code here - and responsibility cannot
be "assigned" under criminal code.  If/when heads roll it will probably be
yours (or close enough to you that it won't matter much).

 



