In article <C45HBH.6I5@rice.edu> swaim@owlnet.rice.edu (Michael Parks Swaim) writes:
>In article <1993Mar16.212215.16227@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>>Can such groups/forums be set up without violating campus rules (how?)
>>Why should/shouldn't a womon join such a group?
>
>  If it's going to be a mailing list, how can the exclude women? I'm not
>talking about legality, I'm talking about mechanics. How can they verify that
>a user's actually female? 

Let me respond as someone who is a member of Systers, a women-only list.
First of all, you should know that the list does not have an automatic
subscribe mechanism.  Women who wish to subscribe send mail to the moderator
who then sends them the rules of the list.  A potential subscriber must
send a statement that she is female and that she agrees to abide by the
rules of the list (which includes keeping messages confidential, only
posting relevant messages, etc.).  The list is built on trust -- trust
that everyone is really who they say they are, and trust that people
will not distribute confidential messages.  So far there has not been
a problem with that. 

The list is not a public list. You could think of it as a list for Anita 
Borg and her friends.  At my university I am free to set up personal 
mailing lists for my friends, classmates, or whatever... and moderate
them however I see fit.  I do not know what the procedure is for adding
a list to the university's listserve.  But I know that if I want to set
up the list myself using the computer resoures normally available to me
as a student, there is no problem with that.  

So I personally don't see any legal problem's with women-only lists.
As for ethical or philisophical problems, the lines appear more fuzzy
to me.  I think the best way I can argue for the need for women-only
lists is by explaining why I joined one.  I attended a science magnet
high school and did my undergraduate degree in an engineering program.
Now I am a graduate student in an engineering program.  It's been eight
years since I've been in a science, math, engineering, or computer class
that had anywhere near the same number of women as men in it.  As an 
undergrad, the only female professor I ever had was for a fine arts
elective I took one semester.  This semester I have a female computer
science professor for the first time ever.  However, I am the only
female student in her class.  I don't have much opportunity to interact
with other women who have similar professional interests.  As a woman
who is currently trying to make educational and career decisions, 
I find it very useful to talk to other women about the factors specific
to women that go into making such decisions.  But until I joined Systers,
I didn't know where to turn to find other women to talk to.  

But what would be the problem of allowing men to be on the Systers list too?
Well, first of all I already get all the input I want from men.
Whenever I want a man's opinion, I know quite a few men who are quite
willing to give me a lenthy lecture.  Second, I feel safer
communicating with large groups of women than I do communicating with large
groups of men or mixed groups.  I spend a lot of time communicating with
men and I have found that I must get used to the fact that I open myself
up for a lot of criticsm (real and in jest) when I do.  You can
argue all you want that men and women do or do not have similar communicatins
styles, but I *know* that I feel safer talking to groups of women
(and I think there are a lot of other women out there who feel the same way).

Well, that's my opinion.....I hope it sheds some light on the issue.

Lorrie 

