>vincent@cad.gatech.edu (Vincent Fox) writes:

>>If K12'ers were reading on my machine, I'd probably have to drop
>>rec.arts.erotica.

>Even at a university site?  Would you drop grownup material only from
>Netnews or would you also remove material from the library, the
>newspaper, and classroom?

Uh my job title is "System Support Specialist I", not Library Manager,
Newspaper Editor, or Teacher. It's not News-Lawyer, News-God, or
Zeus: God of Thunder. Just an ordinary SA trying to keep news cranking
along on an ancient Sun 3/260 with a giant 8" CDC 515 SMD drive.
*Salvaged equipment* since news is not a mission priority.

Look I think you misunderstand the roles of news-admins in this game.
Most managers don't want to be bothered about policy decisions, and *order*
you to make them. Then when the excrement hits the air-mover because
the Dean's daughter was illegally using her dad's account to read
alt.sex.something or even harmless old rec.arts.erotica it's YOUR ASS
that gets dressed down in the Dean's office for it. I'm only allowed
to make broad libertarian decisions in areas where the bosses have
forgotten to tell me what to do or don't understand what's going on.
This is the more normal mode of operation from what I see anyway. The
news-admin carries lots of groups, and either lies to management or
just doesn't tell them what they're up to. 

Do I believe in leaving it all up to the suits? NO! I'll slant
my presentation to leave as many options open as I can, but only
a fool sets up something like say an anon-server and forgets to
ask his boss for an okay on it. Now if I could make a good enough
pack of lies to argue that even though rec.arts.erotica might in
someone's wild imaginings be a threat, that even so it should remain
up, all is well and good. But if despite my pleas they say to remove
it I would do so. To fail to warn them of this problem when it
would be glaringly obvious would be considered negligent by both myself
and most certainly by them.

If K-12'ers had accounts on my machines, you bet your sweet bippy I'd have
to remove rec.arts.erotica. Hell I read it myself on occasion, but the
point here is that your boss wil inevitably say that is what must be
done *at your site*. If you wish to risk being the target of a lawsuit
for pushing pornography on children, be my guest. Most big shops with
the kind of million-dollar equipment sets I'm used to are pretty 
conservative. I would have no problems with doing this on my own machine
either, but when it's government property that's a whole different
matter. After EyeWitless News has "exposed" your computer pornography
ring where unsuspecting 12-year olds had readnews forcibly download
alt.sex.bestiality GIFs onto their PCs *WITHOUT THEM DOING ANYTHING*.....
well you might not like the end results of such "public outrage".

I would note that libraries and classrooms generally have access control
to prevent children from getting hold of a copy of "S&M Monthly". News-programs
I've seen so far have no real capability to control access. Perhaps if
if the access control were written into the server it might work.
Simply putting a check in the reader is too easy to bypass, even accidentally.

Do I like any of this crap and consider it better if all reporters and lawyers
were herded into "showers"? Yep. But I gotta make do with the screwed up system
I got. Things like DOING MY JOB and NOT EMBARASSING MY INSTITUTE rank
pretty high up on my priority list. These are my PRACTICAL considerations.
In theory I'd like to carry all the alt's, in practice I can't. Even
the most vocal 1st amendment reporters will admit that they can write anything
that they want, but this doesn't mean their editor will print it.

