In article <C4Dzo3.MzA@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
>I'm all for passing the buck. One of the best things to do is to
>forwarded to issue to a committee. This is what they did at Stanford,
>U. of Wisconsin at Milwaukee, U. of British Columbia, and U. of
>Waterloo. In every case, the committee recommended reinstating the
>removed newsgroups and using library principles in the selection of
>such computer material. In every case, the President, Chancellor, etc
>accepted these recommendations. (see refs).

This is absolute nonsense.  The alt.binaries.pictures.erotica and
rec.arts.erotica newsgroups have been dropped at literally >dozens< of major
universities which otherwise offer a complete newsfeed.  Not once have any of
these decisions been overturned by an academic committee.  Your counterexample
of the case where Stanford temporarily dropped rec.humor.funny is hardly
comparable.  Besides, the case is now four years old.  Awareness of the scope
of net.abuse, and the possible legal consequences to universities, has grown
significantly among academic computing policy committees since then.

It should also be noted that many other sites which continue to carry the
above-named groups, and which have a need to carry the k12.* groups, choose to
place them on different server machines, so that the participating k12 students
do not (at least in theory) have physical access to the "adult" material.

You continue to claim in your references that the decision at the UW to drop
a.b.p.e was overturned by faculty committee, and that is completely false.  The
group has been banned here for the last 18 months.

It is true that a growing number of schools are making decisions on newsgroup
adoption patterned after existing library policies, which I agree is a Good
Thing.  However, your interpretation of what such policies mean, and your
personal definition of "academic freedom", is radically different from what is
intended by faculty and library staff.  The UW library does not subscribe to
"Hustler" or "Penthouse Letters", and even if they did, they would not grant
access to such material to minors.  I challenge you to name any other major
university that is different.

By defending the use of the academic computer networks to distribute hard-core
pornography to minors, you disgrace the entire cause of "academic freedom".

--
Bill Newell
wcn@u.washington.edu

