** The last 4 reports published by the Home Office dating from 1979 
   onwards have all concluded that pornography should be available
   to adults and that its availability has no effect upon the number
   of sex crimes.

   When Sweeden temporarily experimented with banning pornography 
   outright for a few months during the early 1970s there was a
   considerable increase in the number of sex offences. They immediately
   reversed the experiment.

   It really is time that the antiquated obscene publications legislation
   was repealed. The Section One definition of obscene as material likely 
   to "deprave and corrupt" is totally inadequate and highly misleading.
   Rather, we should consider moving towards a test similar, for example,
   to that used in the State of Iowa (USA). Here the test for obscenity
   is : "patently offensive"

   As a law student it really does annoy me when people talk about 
   the law and obscenity and state that "it should be made tougher"
   or " more strict." Most people know  that an object, thing or publication
   must deprave and corrupt in order to be obscene. However, most members of
   the public do not have any idea about what material has been judged to be
   obscene in the past and where the current boundaries are set.
