Yep, I missed it, but I cry foul.  The "batch/mar_21_1993" header is not
descriptive, and you're misquoting yourself above.  The original article (as I
received it) did not include the words "British Columbia" or "library-like,
netnews policy."  So it was easy to miss.  Credible researchers don't change
their words when quoting, and they learn to use footnotes.

I got the report from UBC and skimmed it (approx. 37 pages!).  I'll make a few
brief comments here.  First, it's a brand new decision, and the report 
acknowledges that several aspects of their decision-making process are without
precedent.  (I can hardly be faulted for not knowing about it.)  Second, it
acknowledges still-active opposition to the decision and alludes to several
possible grounds for legal challenge in BC.  In other words, it isn't final.
Third, much of their position is based on their interpretation of Canadian law,
which is quite different from US law, and so their precedent may be meaningless
here.  Their report cites only one existing US case, at Stanford, which I have
disregarded because it did not address the specific groups in question (a.b.p.e
and r.a.e).

The report also weighs heavily on an argument I've seen kicked around a lot
here, but never presented in a policy document, towit:  Since everyone else on
the net is doing it, and since our users can easily connect to other sites'
servers, we can't block access netwide, so therefore no purpose is served by
blocking access locally.  I do not wish to rehash the debate on that point 
here; I merely state that I disagree.  It is a pivotal argument, one which may
ultimately have to be adjudicated in court(s).  If it comes to that, we will
not see this matter resolved in my lifetime.

What it comes down to is that I still don't see an example of a US university's
computing policy committee overturning a decision to drop either of the two
above-named groups.  Am I missing something else?

>I encourage you to post (or email) the committees' report or
