I never said that the library restricts access to minors.  Quite the opposite,
many matriculated students >are< minors, and they all have full library privs.
Some sexually-oriented material is not kept on the stacks.  The reference
librarians use their discretion whether or not to check a person's age when
such material is requested.  As I said before, some material is held not 
because it is pornographic but because it is "prurient"; i.e. it's legal for
minors to look at it, but the library will take care anyway lest the material
be destroyed.  I don't know if there is a written policy document to back this
up.

Playboy is a hybrid case.  Certain issues have contained material that the
library has felt is at least problematic, and so they've taken greater care in
handling them.  Interestingly, these same issues tend to cause problems in the
private sector, and some stores will put them under the counter where otherwise
they'd be out on a rack.  I don't read the magazine myself, so I can't cite a
recent example by date.  Perhaps someone can help me here.  Most issues of
Playboy, however, do not cause problems with access to minors, and in those
instances the library is motivated to control access solely to protect the
physical property.

In answer to your other question, no, I do not consider Playboy to be
pornographic.

>I'm not sure which ALA policy statement you read, but here are some
>excerpts from some of the ones I've read: (If you believe I'm
>excerpting them unfairly, please document your charge.)

You're excerpting them unfairly in the sense that they have nothing to do with
the issue(s) at hand.  I have no quibble with the "Library Bill of Rights" or
the "Free Access to Libraries for Minors".  The purpose of the latter document
is to prevent discrimination against minors who wish to use the full spectrum
of library services: textbooks, novels, the NYTimes, etc.  This is a laudable
goal.  (I was labelled as "gifted" when I was young, and put ahead in school,
and I've experienced such discrimination first-hand.)  It is >not< intended,
however, to challenge statutory restrictions on the distribution of various
categories of sexually-oriented materials to minors.  To the extent that any
library chooses to carry any such material, it is duty-bound to uphold the law.
I do not believe the ALA has ever intended to dispute that notion, and I think
your citations from their various policy documents radically misrepresent their
organization.  I don't know how I can make it any plainer.

