>This seems to contradict the no censorship policy. I think it would be
>better to forbid only the running of such programs. As a fall back
>position, I think it would be better forbid the object code (but not
>the source code) of such programs (see refs). By way of analogy, most
>university libraries contain books on lock picking and bomb making.
>Although picking university locks and bombing things is illegal,
>instructions on how to do it are not.

Agreed, but the books won't do it for you; a computer program will.

If I have the source code, I can compile/execute/delete it within
a few minutes; the damage is done, I'm back to source-code-only,
and the admins are (or may be) none the wiser.

This is a sticky situation; we (the admins) are trying to balance
the education/experimentation of some users against the (reasonable)
expectation of confidentiality held by other users.  If someone is
running/testing such programs on a one-user box (their desktop work-
station, for example), I wouldn't even think twice about it.  If,
on the other hand, I start seeing peculiar file accesses (in a public
ps listing, for example), I feel that I have to protect the expected
confidentiality of other users.

What would you think about a "please let us know if your work could 
be misconstrued as a violation of our policy"  clause, if it was made
clear that no reasonable work would be refused?  I've worked under 
such a premise for some time, and it can work rather well.  I had a 
few users actively studying cryptography, and they were using cbw 
(Crypt Breaker's Workbench), Crack, and several other tools whose 
use could have been misconstrued as cracking.  They came to me before 
starting their work, just to let me know not to worry.  To the best 
of my knowledge, none of them betrayed my trust.

--Wes

