In a related note, I just read in this morning's _Lexington Herald-Leader_
that the Lexington Public Library's board has decided that certain materials
will not be made available to minors without parental permission.  Those
materials include the library's subscription to _Playboy_ magazine, videos
rated NC-17 and R (I don't believe the library holds any X-rated videos), 
and Madonna's near-infamous _Sex_.

This brings up an interesting (to me, anyway) question.  Many of us have
said that this a *parental* decision, rather than an institutional one.
What's wrong (if anything *is* wrong) with a policy that requires parental 
input?  Isn't it proper to place the decision where it should have been
all along -- in the hands of the parents?

Personally,  I don't want someone else overriding my decisions about my
child's reading/viewing of adult materials.  When I believe that my child
is ready for such things, I'll make them available, regardless of the
child's age at the time.  However, it isn't the public library's (or the
school's, or the church's) place to try to guess my wishes, or to override
them in the name of some nebulous 'freedom to read.'  

When I was 8, my parents would have been horrified if the local library 
provided me with materials like _Sex_ or even _Playboy_ without their 
knowledge and/or consent; by the time I was 10, they would have been 
horrified if the library had refused me those same materials.  I realize 
that every child is different, but who is in the best position to make
those judgements?  The parents, that's who.

I see nothing wrong with a policy that places sensitive discussions (and
sensitive materials) back in the parental/family fold.  I've watched the
meetings of the local library board and school board; those folks don't
represent me or my values very well, and I don't think I want them to
determine what my child can or cannot read.  Let me know what's available,
and my wife and I will make those decisions until we believe that our
child is ready to make those decisions herself; at that time, I'll happily
sign an 'unlimited access' request for her.

--Wes

ps> Of course, my daughter is -5 weeks of age, so this may just be
    father-to-be ranting.   8)


