>>Does Iowa State University ban textual materials dealing with sex from its
>>libraries?
>
>No - but they exercise "acquisition control".

Are you aware that there is a distinction between acquisition and
divestment? No one is talking about forcing a university to *add* 
newsgroups. What we're talking about is a university's decision to 
cease carrying newsgroups for silly or unprincipled reasons.

> I doubt they would allocate
>significant shelf space for the public to deposit "kiddie-porn" (or "adult
>porn", for that matter) at will, to be checked out through the library's
>services.

I doubt that this comment is relevant. No one's talking about child-porn
newsgroups.

> I doubt they would distribute articles published as copyright
>violations - no matter how popular they were.  

Are you under the impression that copyright violations on Usenet occur
only in the alt.sex hierarchy?

>Ah - so it is NOT a first amendment right being violated by Usenet
>"acquisition control" - and it is not censorship (a point I contend). 

No one is talking about "acquisition control," so your point here is a non
sequitur. See my comment above. Moreover, the issue of First Amendment
applicability is hardly the same issue as censorship. The First Amendment,
by its terms, limits government action; the word "censorship" may be
applied both to government and private action. (When it's government
action, we call it "government censorship.")

>It
>is an "academic freedom" issue.  I will also contend that "academic freedom"
>comes with responsible use - and you are not allowed to violate law with it.

Dear Mr. Kunz,

I am a lawyer. Which laws are violated by the textual newsgroups dealing
with sexuality? I would be deeply interested in supplementing my legal 
knowledge with your insight in this area since, at present, I am unaware 
of any issue of "violating the law" connected with a university's
carrying, say, alt.sex.bondage.

>>The issue can't be obscenity, since the textual groups are almost
>>certainly not legally obscene.
>
>From the Iowa Code, Chapter 728 - "Obscenity", section 728.1, "Definitions":
>
>    2.  "Material" means any book, magazine, newspaper or other printed or
>  written material or any picture, drawing, photograph, motion picture, or
>  other pictorial representation or any statue or other figure, or any
>  recording, transcription or mechanical, chemical or electrical reproduction
>  or any other articles, equipment, machines or materials.

Sadly, you seem to be unfamiliar with obscenity law. You falsely conclude
that the fact that textual materials *can* be obscene implies that the
textual newsgroups dealing with sexuality *are* obscene. This kind of
confusion would earn you a bad mark on a law-school exam. My advice is
that you take the trouble to read something of the history of the law of
obscenity in America. Start with Edward de Grazia's GIRLS LEAN BACK
EVERYWHERE: THE LAW OF OBSCENITY AND THE ASSAULT ON GENIUS (1992). De
Grazia, a law professor at Cardozo Law School in NYC, provides ample
explanations of modern obscenity law that might clear up the kind of
conceptual confusion you demonstrate here.

In the meantime, here's a broad hint: My comment was not that the textual
groups are not legally obscene *because they are text*. Instead, I simply
said they were almost certainly not legally obscene--that is, that they
would probably not be declared obscene under the Supreme Court's obscenity
test in Miller v. California.

>The library runs under ALA's standards.  Usenet has no standards
>organization (although I have contended in other posts it needs one to
>control itself).

How would such a standards organization impose its standards? Are you
under the impression that ALA has an enforcement arm?

> You are trying to depict an applicability of ALA's
>standards to a non-organized group of computer systems which have a large
>quantity of violations the ALA would find objectionable.

This comment suggests you are unfamiliar with the ALA. If you knew about
Judith Krug's work for ALA's Freedom to Read project, you wouldn't be so
quick to say what ALA would find objectionable. It's dead-solid certain
that ALA wouldn't try to ban the kinds of discussions and fiction that
appear in alt.sex.bondage.

> Usenet is not a
>library.

But library principles may well be applicable to universities' Usenet
policies. This may be true regardless of whether Usenet is in fact a
library. See, e.g., Stanford's decision to refer the issue of whether to
carry rec.humor.funny to the university council's library committee.
Bet you didn't know about that one.

> The ALA's standards do not apply to newspapers or broadcasting
>media.

Sure they do. ALA principles (not "standards," technically, since they
can't be enforced) apply to university libraries that make decisions about
what newspapers and videotaped programs to stock.

> In a similar fashion they do not automatically apply to a "computer
>broadcasting" media such as Usenet. 

It would be helpful if you did some research into the history of
broadcasting regulation before you resort to the argument-by-assertion
that Usenet is the equivalent of broadcasting. I'll give you sources, if
you like.



