From:  Peter Graham, Rutgers University Libraries

Let me ask for comments on this analogy.  If I were at a public
meeting on, say stamp collecting or local city governance, and a
speaker came to the microphone with a mask on or otherwise concealing
identity, I would be curious and slightly concerned; I might or might
not take the opinions voiced very seriously, but I think would tend to
take them less seriously than if it were an identified member of the
community.

If a series of several people similarly masked came to the microphone
and voiced similar views, of whatever kind, I would feel it desirable
for the public good to know who this group was, and in addition why it
felt it necessary to mask itself.  I would assert that it is damaging
to the quality of public debate, on whatver topic, for parties
contributing to it not wanting to be known; and that there is prima
facie evidence of not wanting to be held intellectually accountable in
debate for their own views, which in a democracy is not normally 
desirable nor acceptable (consider also the dim view generally held of
anonymous letters to the editor except for specific personal
protection reasons).

I suggest also that these cases are different from the private meeting
of people who may wish to share explicitly personal experiences in a
protective environment, such as we know of A.A., battered wife groups,
and the like.   (There may be nuances of overflow between one and the
other circumstance I've laid out here, but a well-meaning person will
I think see the broad difference.)

While anonymous postings to a newsgroup of the latter kind might be
desirable and encouraged, why should anonymous postings to newsgroups
of a general, public nature be allowed or encouraged?  In what way is
such a situation different from the public meeting I described above?
Why should contributions to open, public debate on non-personal topics
be anonymous?

