A major difference between Usenet and a library is that Usenet allows
someone outside your system (even outside your state or country) to place
items on it that may violate local policy or any laws that apply to your
site.  This is something libraries do not allow.  For example, libraries
would not allow patrons to photocopy copyrighted books and place them on the
shelves for circulation by their circ department.  Usenet needs a similar
operating standard.

>[referring ALA guidelines and Usenet]
>[...]  It is highly deceptive
>of you to continually cite such statements from professional organizations out
>of context.  I do not believe the ALA has ever produced a policy recommendation
>covering any part of Usenet.

Agreed.  Some Usenet sites have adopted standards similar to ALA to apply to
their systems.  It is local policy.  I believe, however, that Usenet is a
"broadcast media" more than a library.  You cannot superimpose ALA's
standards over Usenet any more than you can apply them to the phone company
or your local radio station.  There may be pieces of ALA doctrine that may
be valuable, however.

>Does anyone out there take Carl Kadie seriously??

I take him seriously.  I have been following censorship and access issues
for several years now.  Many of the things he is fighting for are valid
issues.  I have talked with system admins at conferences who feel they have
a right to look at, purge, or alter any files on their systems belonging to
their users ("It is my hardware, therefore it is my file").  I equate this
with wiretapping or breaking-and-entering.  There indeed are rights for
users that need protection.  

I feel, however, that the "defacto" standard many on the net are applying to
Usenet news to allow anything and everything is misleading and dangerous.
Over the past couple years I have come to the following convictions:

  1) Usenet News is not "above the law".

  2) Usenet News is not a "library" and ALA standards need not apply.
     Usenet is a computer-technology "broadcast media".

  3) Acquisition control by a local Usenet site based on the law, 
     system resources, or the "mission" of the institution/organization 
     is not censorship.  Other selection criteria may be censorship.
     Acquisition control should be applied uniformly when applied.

  4) Each Usenet site needs a written local policy concerning Usenet
     News.  This policy should be formed in a formal manner by whatever 
     means that institution/organization normally uses to form policy for
     key operating aspects.  The policy should be available to all users.

  5) Usenet is in need of an "operating standards" organization with the
     task of controlling such problematic aspects as copyright infringement,
     child-pornography, and the like.  Without a means of Usenet peers 
     controlling themselves, outside control will follow (probably at the
     network level).

  6) (Last but not least) Usenet News is valuable enough to worry about.
     It is a key asset for an Internet-connected site.

