In article <1213@ulogic.UUCP>, hartman@ulogic.UUCP (Richard M. Hartman) writes:
> In article <skunz.732903675@corvette.cc.iastate.edu> skunz@iastate.edu (Steven L Kunz) writes:
> :Yes - reviewing the contents of all groups is impossible.  Making no attempt
> :at control, however, is foolish from a legal standpoint.
> 
> Not so sure about that ... I think that if you make some efforts
> at control, but miss things or abdicate responsibility for some
> subset, you are actually liable for more if you make no efforts
> at control and clearly state that policy.

I see three possible scenarios:

1. YOU CHECK EVERYTHING - If you take at least a glance at everything
that comes and goes on your BBS, then you're going to be liable for
whatever gets carried (since you okayed it). But then presumably you
wouldn't okay non-legit stuff, so this should put you on fairly safe
legal grounds, except when it comes to things like libel which can be
difficult even for experts to recognize in advance.

2. YOU CHECK SOME THINGS - This can happen one of two ways: either
you do a rigorous but random check of contents (say one in every 25
postings), or you sign on evey once in awhile and check a few postings
to see what's there. Now, I still think checking everything is your
best bet, but if you can't do it for some reason (such as the volume
of postings on Usenet), then I think establishing a system for
checking is the safest course of action. That's provided, of course,
that your system is really designed to catch offenders rather than
excuse you from liability for offenses. In other words, if you can
prove that you made a good faith effort to keep problem postings off
your bbs, you're on better ground than if your only excuse is, "Oh,
I'd sign on every once in awhile and check a posting or two, but I
didn't ever see anything wrong." Of course, even a good system may not
get you completely off the hook, but I believe you'll be in less
trouble than if...

3. YOU CHECK NOTHING - If you pay no attention to how your system is
used, *especially* if you *deliberately* set it up so that it's
*impossible* for you to monitor postings, then here's how your trial
is going to go:

Prosecutor: So let me make sure I understand this...You set the system
up so that there was no way for you to check who was using it or what
they were doing with it?

You: That's correct.

Prosecutor: In full knowledge of the possibility that child
pornographers and God-only-knows what other kind of scum might use
your system for illegal traffic, you deliberately provided a computer
system for public access where no end of kiddie porn could be
distributed and nobody, not even you, could stop it?

What would your answer be to this? That you didn't think criminals
would use your system? That's a pretty poor excuse, especially if crimes
in fact took place (and if you're on trial, it did).

Again, in legal terms this is called a "dangerous instrumentality,"
and you *will* be held liable for creating it.

-Bryan
