Hold on a second!  Step back!  Take a deep breath....in.......out.

As I recall, it is not illegal to be a member of the KKK.  If they
want to set up an email site and pull down a feed, they can use the
'tax-supported Internet' just as legally as you and I.  If they want
real-time connectivity, Dial'n'CERF, Flyin' Tiger, and CIX would be
happy to give them such connections.  Their materials will continue
to fly over the 'tax-supported Internet.'

Side question:  what's the difference between nyx.cs.du.edu and
engr.uky.edu?  None, right?  Both are machines at educational
institutions, and both are (probably) publicly-owned machines.
Does the fact that nyx.cs.du.edu offers access to the general
public strike you a misuse of the 'tax-funded Internet'?  If so,
why?  If not, why not?  In either case, what's the difference?

I guess the next argument will be that they are using publicly-owned
systems to propagate their mailing list.  In a preventive defense, let
me just state that the publicly-owned system should be allowed to pro-
vide such services, AS LONG AS they can provide them equitably to all 
who request them.  (This is roughly akin to the common carrier law's 
requirement that the business accept all proper customers.)

When one of my users asks to set up a mailing list, my total involvement
consists of about 15 minutes' explanation and three lines in sendmail.cf:

    userslist: :include:/home/joeuser/userslist/member-list
    userslist-request: joeuser
    userslist-owner: joeuser

Joe User goes and runs his list, and I'm out of the loop.  Again, I'm
merely *providing a service*; as long as I provide equitable service
to all who request it, I'm not in any hot water.  If the list engages
in illegal topics, the list owner will be brought to heel by the 
appropriate people. (by the way, I am *not* the appropriate person.)

Did you notice anything missing?  Did you pick up on the fact that I 
don't ask about topics?  Who cares?  I'm just *providing a service*.
The telephone company doesn't ask if my 900 number will provide phone
sex or football scores..........

I'll also point out that it is quite possible for a user to run a 
mailing list without the admin knowing about it.  (unless the admin
pays close attention to the mailer logs)  In fact, my 'standing offer'
came about after I noticed a user sending email to 28 people on a 
regular basis. (some of the addresses were rather peculiar; that's
the only reason they caught my eye several times)  That user doesn't
burn up more CPU time than anyone else, so what's the problem?  I
have no idea what that list discusses, nor do I care.  I'm just
*providing a service*.

Were such a mailing list managed by University staff, I'd start
to wonder about it; as long as a private user is doing the job,
I'm just *providing a service*.  

--Wes

